# SOUL.md — template (structure only)

The real file lives in `$BRAIN_DIR/personal/SOUL.md`. It is not in this repo
and should not be.

It reaches the agent by copy, never by symlink:

    bash scripts/install-agent-prompts.sh

The agent runs as `brain` and cannot read `$BRAIN_DIR`, so a symlink resolves
to something it is not allowed to open. The destination is the agent's
*workspace* (`~/.openclaw/workspace/`), not `~/.openclaw/`, which holds config
and credentials. OpenClaw reads this file at the start of every session, so a
re-run takes effect on the next message with no gateway restart.

Budget: 20,000 characters (`agents.defaults.bootstrapMaxChars`). Over that,
OpenClaw truncates silently. Short beats long here anyway.

This file is **voice, stance and style**. OpenClaw's guidance is to keep
operating rules in `AGENTS.md` and personality here; both load every session.

Sections worth having:

## Purpose
What this assistant is for, in one paragraph. The answer is in `README.md` and
`prompts/routing-policy.md`: it is the local tier, and it gets everything that
touches personal context — by data sensitivity, not by difficulty.

## The seam
It returns decisions and artifacts across tiers, never a digest of personal
information for a frontier model to consume. See `prompts/routing-policy.md`.

## Voice
How it should talk to me. Concision, directness, when to push back.

## Uncertainty
What to do when it does not know, and when a tool fails. Default: say so and
stop. This is the section that stops confabulation — the failure actually
observed on 2026-09-06, when it described a workspace that did not exist.

## Boundaries
What it must never do without asking.

**None of this is enforcement.** There is no root-level `approval` key in
OpenClaw; everything here is a prompt that a 27B local model may ignore. Actions
are gated by `tools.allow` / `tools.deny` in `config/openclaw.json.template`,
and that is the only place a constraint is actually binding. Write the intent
here; enforce it there.
